  "n": 1,
  "title": "Why Baker Drops Technical Indicators",
  "kind": "article",
  "summary": "Chapter 1 argues that indicators are not where trading edge comes from. Leading oscillators (RSI, Stochastic) throw false signals, lagging ones (MACD, moving averages) get you in late, and the few indicator traders who make money do so because of risk management and plan discipline. Indicators also make you predictable to professionals who hunt the stops the crowd places. The book's alternative: read supply and demand directly from price and put stops beyond the zones.",
  "sections": [
   {
    "heading": "What indicators are and why they disappoint",
    "points": [
     "Indicators are decision aids for entries and exits: oscillators, moving averages, trend lines and more complex formulas - well over 100 of them.",
     "Two families: leading (oscillators such as RSI and Stochastic) and lagging (anything built from moving averages, such as MACD).",
     "Lagging indicators get you into the trade late; leading indicators fire false signals.",
     "Learning them all costs time and money and leaves you not much better off - a busy-looking screen does not make a better trader.",
     "Weather analogy: forecasting crunches huge amounts of data and gets worse the further ahead it looks; Baker says his local forecasts are right about half the time. Indicator forecasting has the same problem."
    ]
   },
   {
    "heading": "What really makes the profitable minority profitable",
    "points": [
     "Baker accepts that some indicator traders make money consistently, but calls them the exception.",
     "Their edge is risk management and sticking to a trading plan, not the indicator.",
     "Thought experiment: a trader with the same strict risk rules whose whole plan is 'buy pullbacks in uptrends, short pullbacks in downtrends' could get the same results.",
     "If you are experienced and like your indicators, you can keep them and add the zone rules from this book to raise your hit rate."
    ]
   },
   {
    "heading": "Indicators as superstition",
    "points": [
     "Craps example: some gamblers pull their bets when the dealers change, then credit the new dealers if a seven comes. The odds of a seven stay 16.7% (one in six) no matter who deals.",
     "Same behaviour in trading: a trader buys on a MACD crossover, credits the crossover when it works and blames something else when it does not.",
     "Psychology names it confirmation bias and gambler's fallacy - acting as if a ritual controls a random outcome."
    ]
   },
   {
    "heading": "Stop hunters",
    "points": [
     "Professionals know what the popular indicators are saying and where the crowd's stops sit.",
     "They also know where the nearest supply and demand zones are. If the zone is far enough from current price, they have the capital to push price through the crowd's stops and get filled at a better price.",
     "The classic experience - stopped out right before price turns and goes your way - is often exactly this.",
     "Defence: always put the stop below a demand zone (long) or above a supply zone (short). That is the bridge into the rest of the book."
    ]
   }
  ],
  "rules": [
   "Do not build entries on indicator signals; read supply and demand from price.",
   "Treat risk management and plan discipline as the real edge, whatever method you use.",
   "Place every stop beyond a zone (below demand for longs, above supply for shorts), never at the obvious indicator or swing level.",
   "If you keep an indicator, it must sit on top of the zone rules, not replace them."
  ],
  "mistakes": [
   "Collecting indicators (100+ exist) instead of learning where supply and demand are.",
   "Entering on a lagging crossover after the move has largely happened.",
   "Crediting the indicator for winners and blaming outside events for losers (confirmation bias).",
   "Parking stops where every indicator trader parks them, making them easy to hunt."
  ],
  "quotes": [
   "Lagging indicators get you into the trade late and leading indicators are prone to false signals.",
   "The best professional traders stick to their trading plan and never deviate from it.",
   "By always placing your stop below a demand zone or above a supply zone, it makes you more immune to stop hunters."
  ],
