"191.9\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"544\" y=\"171.3\" width=\"7\" height=\"19.3\" fill=\"#ef4444\" rx=\"1\"/><line x1=\"559\" y1=\"186.7\" x2=\"559\" y2=\"204.3\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"555.5\" y=\"190.6\" width=\"7\" height=\"3.9\" fill=\"#ef4444\" rx=\"1\"/><text x=\"530\" y=\"318\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">3. Stop beyond the nearest zone</text><text x=\"530\" y=\"333\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" text-anchor=\"middle\">too much risk = no trade</text></svg>",
    "caption": "The three rules Baker says are worth the price of the book even if you keep your current strategy: never buy with a supply zone directly overhead, never short with a demand zone directly underneath (in both cases wait for the break or pass), and place the stop a few cents beyond the nearest zone - if that is too much risk, there is no trade."
